 # tally — Frequently Asked Questions
 
-## Is tally really free?
+## Getting started
+
+### Is tally really free?
 
 Yes. Running tally on your own computer is free and always will be. The only thing
 that costs money is the optional hosted backup add-on.
 
-## How many expenses can I track on the free plan?
+### What do I need to install?
+
+Nothing beyond Python 3.9 or newer. Clone the repository and run:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli --help
+```
+
+If you want to type `tally` instead of the full module path, add a shell alias:
+
+```
+alias tally="python -m tally.cli"
+```
+
+### How do I record my first expense?
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli add 12.50 food --note "lunch"
+```
+
+The first argument is the amount, the second is the category. A note is optional
+but useful later when you are trying to remember what a charge was.
+
+---
+
+## Data & privacy
+
+### Where is my data kept?
+
+In a single JSON file on your own machine (`tally.json` in whatever directory you
+run the command from). Unless you turn on the hosted backup add-on, your data never
+leaves your computer.
+
+### Can I look at the raw data?
+
+Yes — it is a plain JSON file and you can open it in any text editor. Each expense
+record includes the date, the amount you entered, the original currency, the
+converted USD amount, the category, and any note you added.
+
+### Can I back up or move my data?
+
+Copy the `tally.json` file. That is the entire state. You can move it to another
+machine, put it in a folder under version control, or open it in a spreadsheet
+after exporting to CSV.
+
+### Can I use a different file name or location?
+
+Yes. Pass `--store` before the subcommand:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli --store ~/finances/2024.json add 50 food
+```
+
+Every command honours `--store`, so you can maintain several separate files —
+one per year, one per project, whatever makes sense for you.
+
+---
+
+## Entering expenses
+
+### What categories can I use?
+
+The built-in categories are: `food`, `transport`, `rent`, `entertainment`, and
+`other`. These are defined in `tally/config.py`. If none of them fit, use `other`
+or edit the config to add your own.
+
+### Can I enter expenses in other currencies?
+
+Yes. Pass `--currency` when you add an expense:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli add 39 food --currency EUR --note "groceries"
+```
+
+Supported currencies are **EUR**, **GBP**, **JPY**, **INR**, and **SGD**, in
+addition to the default **USD**. The amount is converted to US dollars immediately
+and that converted figure is what gets stored. Your original amount and currency
+are kept alongside it for reference.
+
+### Why does tally convert to USD straight away?
+
+Consistency. If you mix currencies across a month, tally needs a single unit to
+add them together. Converting at the time you record means every total and report
+you ever see is computed the same way — no surprises when a rate changes later.
+
+### Can I backdate an expense?
+
+Yes. Pass `--date` in `YYYY-MM-DD` format:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli add 45 transport --date 2024-03-15 --note "train"
+```
+
+If you omit `--date`, today's date is used.
+
+### What if I make a mistake?
+
+Edit `tally.json` directly in a text editor. Each expense has an `"id"` field so
+you can find the right one easily. There is no delete command in the CLI at the
+moment, so the JSON file is the escape hatch.
+
+---
+
+## Viewing and exporting data
+
+### How do I see where my money went?
+
+The `report` command prints category totals for a month:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli report --month 2024-03
+```
+
+Add `--category` to narrow it to one category:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli report --month 2024-03 --category food
+```
+
+Omit `--month` to see totals across all time.
+
+### How do I see individual expenses instead of totals?
+
+Use the `list` command. It accepts the same `--month` and `--category` filters:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli list --month 2024-03
+```
+
+### How do I get overall statistics?
+
+The `stats` command prints your total expense count, all-time spend, average per
+expense, and the date range covered:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli stats
+```
+
+### How do I check spending against my budget?
+
+The `budget` command compares this month's spending to the limits set in
+`tally/config.py`:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli budget --month 2024-03
+```
+
+It flags categories where you have used 80 % or more of the budget.
+
+### How do I export to a spreadsheet?
+
+Use the `export` command. Without `--out` it prints CSV to the terminal; with
+`--out` it writes a file:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli export --out march.csv
+```
+
+All amounts in the export are in US dollars.
+
+---
+
+## Plans and backup
+
+### How many expenses can I track on the free plan?
+
+You can record as many expenses as you like locally — there is no local limit.
+The free plan covers hosted backup for up to **100 expenses per month**. If you
+record more than that, the extra expenses are still saved on your machine; they
+are just not included in the hosted backup until you upgrade.
+
+### What are the plan options?
+
+| Plan | Price | Expenses backed up per month | Backup history |
+|------|-------|------------------------------|----------------|
+| Free | $0 | up to 100 | 7 days |
+| Plus | $3 / month | unlimited | 1 year |
+| Family | $6 / month | unlimited | 3 years |
+
+See `docs/pricing.md` for full details.
+
+### Is there an annual billing option?
+
+Not yet. All plans are billed monthly.
+
+### When do plan changes take effect?
+
+Upgrades and downgrades take effect at the start of the next billing month.
+
+---
+
+## Scope and limits
+
+### Does tally handle recurring expenses, split bills, or tax categories?
 
-The free plan includes hosted backup for up to **100 expenses per month**. You can
-record more than that locally — nothing stops you — but only the first 100 in a
-month are covered by the free backup. The Plus and Family plans remove the limit.
+No. tally is a personal tool for tracking what you spend, not an accounting
+package. If you need recurring entries, shared budgets, or tax reporting, you have
+outgrown tally.
 
-## Where is my data kept?
+### What if I want to track spending in a non-USD currency?
 
-In a single JSON file on your own machine. Unless you turn on the hosted backup
-add-on, your data never leaves your computer.
+tally converts everything to USD, so reports are always in USD. If you consistently
+spend in another currency and want reports in that currency, tally is not the right
+tool — but the raw JSON and CSV export give you the original amounts to work with.
 
-## Can I enter expenses in other currencies?
+### Can I use tally for business expenses?
 
-Yes. Pass `--currency` when you add an expense and tally converts it to US dollars
-for you.
+Technically yes, but it was not designed for that. There is no receipt attachment,
+no approval workflow, and no multi-user access. For personal use it works well.
